6. Important distinction for AI vulnerabilities
Not every AI behavior should automatically become a vulnerability.
Observation	Potential classification
Chatbot reveals another user's trade	High/Critical — authorization/data leakage
Chatbot reveals internal system prompt	Usually Low/Medium, depending on sensitive content and impact
Chatbot follows malicious instructions in a trade note and retrieves unauthorized data	High/Critical — indirect prompt injection leading to data leakage
Chatbot makes an incorrect financial calculation	Business logic / AI reliability, depending on impact
Chatbot hallucinates a nonexistent trade	AI reliability/data integrity; severity depends on actual impact
Chatbot refuses a jailbreak	Not a vulnerability
Chatbot reveals generic model configuration	Usually informational unless sensitive information is exposed
Verbose mode displays API credentials	High/Critical depending on credential privilege
Changing a model parameter bypasses backend permissions	Security vulnerability
Chatbot returns unauthorized data despite UI restrictions	Security vulnerability; server-side authorization failure

The key rule: Raise AI security findings when you demonstrate a real security impact—not just because the model gives an undesirable answer.

